  "OverallSummary": "In this case, Alfred S. Vanderlaan willfully violated Regulation Best Interest (Reg BI) and FINRA Rule 2010 by recommending GWG L Bonds to two retail customers when these recommendations were not in the customers' best interests. GWG L Bonds were speculative, unrated corporate bonds with a high degree of risk that eventually defaulted in January 2022, leading to GWG's bankruptcy in April 2022. The Care Obligation violations involved inappropriate investment recommendations relative to clients' profiles. For both customers, Vanderlaan recommended high-risk investments that were inconsistent with their moderate risk tolerances and growth and income investment objectives (which did not include speculation). The inappropriate recommendations led to excessive concentration in risky alternative investments: Customer A, a senior investor with moderate risk tolerance, had over 20% of liquid net worth in alternative investments, while Customer B had 23% of liquid net worth in alternative investments (with 21% in GWG L Bonds specifically). FINRA's analysis focused on: 1) The customers' stated risk tolerance (moderate) versus the actual risk of the product (high); 2) The customers' stated investment objectives (growth and income, not speculation) versus the speculative nature of the product; 3) The concentration levels of high-risk investments in the customers' portfolios following the recommendations. Vanderlaan earned $6,508 in commissions from these unsuitable recommendations. As sanctions, he received a three-month suspension, $10,000 fine, and was ordered to pay partial restitution of $6,508 plus interest to the affected customers."
